+\section{Numerical algorithm}\label{sec:numerics}
+
+We describe the algorithm used to compute the discrete sweeping sequence $(X^n)_{n\geq 0}$
+defined by \eqref{eq.sweeping.projection}.
+Given the kernel $\kernel$, the time step $h>0$,
+the past condition $X_p \in \Lip(\RR^-)$,
+and a family of constraint sets $(C^n)_{n\geq 0}$,
+the scheme proceeds as follows.
+
+\subsection{Time-stepping scheme}
+
+For $n = 0, 1, \ldots, N$ with $N = \lfloor T/h \rfloor$:
+\begin{compactenum}[1)]
+	\item Compute the discrete kernel weights:
+	$R_j = \frac{1}{h}\int_{jh}^{(j+1)h} \kernel(a)\, da$,
+	truncated at $j = J_{\max}$ where $R_{J_{\max}} < \texttt{tol}$.
+	\item Compute the weighted average of past positions:
+	\begin{equation}\label{eq.num.xbar}
+		\overline{X}^n = \frac{h \sum_{j=1}^{J_{\max}} R_j\, X^{n-j}}{h \sum_{j=1}^{J_{\max}} R_j},
+	\end{equation}
+	where $X^{n-j} = X_p((n-j)h)$ whenever $n-j < 0$.
+	\item Compute the projection onto the constraint set:
+	$X^n = P_{C^n}(\overline{X}^n)$.
+\end{compactenum}
+
+\noindent For the exponential kernel $\kernel(a) = \varepsilon e^{-\varepsilon a}$,
+the weights admit the closed form
+$R_j = \frac{1}{h}(1 - e^{-\varepsilon h}) e^{-\varepsilon j h}$
+and the truncation index satisfies $J_{\max} = \lceil -\ln(\texttt{tol})/(\varepsilon h) \rceil$.
+
+\subsection{Projection via Newton's method}
+
+When the constraint set is defined implicitly as $C^n = \{x \in \RR^d : g_n(x) \geq 0\}$
+for a smooth concave function $g_n$, the projection $P_{C^n}(\overline{X}^n)$
+coincides with the projection onto the boundary $\{g_n = 0\}$ whenever
+$g_n(\overline{X}^n) < 0$ (i.e.\ $\overline{X}^n \notin C^n$).
+
+We compute this projection using Newton's method along the gradient direction.
+Starting from $p_0 = \overline{X}^n$, we iterate for $k = 0, 1, \ldots$:
+\begin{equation}\label{eq.newton}
+	p_{k+1} = p_k - \frac{g_n(p_k)}{|\nabla g_n(p_k)|^2}\, \nabla g_n(p_k),
+\end{equation}
+until $|g_n(p_k)| < \texttt{tol}_{\text{proj}}$,
+and set $X^n = p_k$.
+If $g_n(\overline{X}^n) \geq 0$, no projection is needed and $X^n = \overline{X}^n$.
+
+The gradient $\nabla g_n$ is approximated by central finite differences:
+$$
+\partial_{x_i} g_n(p) \approx \frac{g_n(p + \delta e_i) - g_n(p - \delta e_i)}{2\delta}
+$$
+with $\delta = 10^{-6}$, where $e_i$ denotes the $i$-th canonical basis vector.
+
+\begin{remark}
+	The iteration \eqref{eq.newton} is well defined whenever
+	$\nabla g_n(p_k) \neq 0$, which is guaranteed for qualified constraints
+	near the boundary.
+	For convex sets with smooth boundary, each step decreases the distance
+	to the boundary and convergence is quadratic near $\{g_n = 0\}$.
+	In practice,
+	$\texttt{tol}_{\text{proj}} = 10^{-8}$
+	and at most $50$ iterations suffice.
+\end{remark}
+
